      "problem": "Factor $-z^3-25z.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's factor $-z$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n-z^3-25z =-z(z^2+25).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $\\left( z^2+25\\right).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using $\\textrm{i}^2 = -1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+25 & ={z}^{2}-(-25) \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-(25{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-(5\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(z+5\\text{i})(z-5\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n-z(z^2+25)=-z(z+5\\textrm i)(z-5\\textrm i).\n$$",
